1. Purpose and case introduction
--------------------------------
The purpose of the present calculation cases was to verify the water coolant channel module of 
OFFBEAT against the U.S. NRC system code TRACE for relevant single rod cases. The rod cases include 
two boiling water reactor (ABWR, BWR4) and two pressurized water reactor (AP1000, VVER440) scenarios
(BWR4 and VVER440 are Generation II, ABWR is Generation III and AP1000 is Generation III+ design).
The used power histories simulated steady-state and slow transient scenarios.


2. Model description
--------------------
Each TRACE model consists of a FILL, a PIPE and a BREAK component. The wall of the PIPE component is 
heated by a HEAT STRUCTURE unit, which is powered by a POWER component. Identically to the OFFBEAT 
cases, the TRACE models are divided into 11 axial nodes, where the bottom 10 nodes have the same 
length, while the top plenum node has a fixed length and is not powered. The dimensions (hydraulic 
diameter, volume, flow area etc.) and the boundaries (power, mass flow rate, pressure history etc.) 
are identical in the corresponding OFFBEAT and TRACE models.


3. Scenario description
-----------------------
The applied hypothetical power scenario was designed to cover a wide range of water boiling regimes. 
It consist of the following phases:
	   I.     0 -  3600 s: The inlet temperature and the system pressure are raised linearly to 
	                       their nominal values, while the power is increased linearly from 0% to 
	                       25% nominal power.
	  II.  3600 -  7200 s: The rod is kept at the values corresponding to t = 3600 s.
	 III.  7200 - 10800 s: The power is raised linearly to its nominal level.
	  IV. 10800 - 14400 s: The rod is kept at the values corresponding to t = 10800 s.
	   V. 14400 - 18000 s: The power is raised linearly to the 200% of the nominal level.
	  VI. 18000 - 21600 s: The rod is kept at the values corresponding to t = 18000 s.
	 VII. 21600 - 25200 s: The power is raised linearly to the 300% of the nominal level for the 
	                       ABWR and BWR4. For the VVER440 and AP1000 cases the final power levels 
	                       had to be increased to 325% and decreased to 240% of the corresponding 
	                       nominal powers, respectively, to ensure the entering of the film boiling 
	                       regime and preventing clad failure.
	VIII. 25200 - 28000 s: The rod is kept at the values corresponding to t = 25200 s.


4. Results and discussion
-------------------------
Absolute wall temperature and wall temperature differences can be visualized with the included
"analyzeResults.py" Python script, which also computes the maximum and average difference between
the OFFBEAT and TRACE wall temperatures at a given time point. In general, the calculated OFFBEAT 
and TRACE wall temperatures agree within a few Kelvins up to the departure from nucleate 
boiling (DNB). The time of DNB generally agree within 8 minutes. The differences are mainly 
attributed to the different qualities (the enthalpy model in OFFBEAT uses the homogeneous 
equilibrium assumption, while TRACE solves the six equations for two fluids).

Significant differences were observed between the film boiling temperatures calculated by the two
codes. In general, the heat transfer correlations obtained from different film boiling correlations
might vary significantly. The differences in wall temperature results obtained for the BWR4 and ABWR
cases correspond to the expected magnitude (i.e. 1-100 K). However, for the VVER440 and AP1000 cases
the differences in film boiling temperatures were above 500 K, with TRACE yielding unexpectedly high
(> 1500 K) wall temperatures. 

Sensitivity calculations were carried out with TRACE and it was found that within a few W of power
variation the top heated node of the pipe either stays in saturated boiling regimes or jumps to 
film boiling with wall temperature exceeding 1500 K, which phenomenon does not seem physical. 
By changing the system pressure, the post-CHF wall temperature gets lower. The possible explanation 
was found in the involved heat transfer coefficient correlations.

In TRACE, three sub-regimes for film boiling are considered: 
   I. For void fractions lower than 0.6 --> inverted annular film boiling (IAFB)
  II. For void fractions higher than 0.9 --> dispersed flow film boiling (DFFB)
 III. For void fractions between 0.6 and 0.9 --> interpolation is carried out between the 
      correlations of IAFB and DFFB.

When the BWR cases enter film boiling, their void fraction is above 0.9; therefore they enter the 
DFFB sub-regime. On the other hand, when the PWR cases enter the film boiling, their void fraction 
is around 0.4, therefore, they will enter the IAFB sub-regime. The correlation used for obtaining 
the heat transfer coefficient of IAFB is validated to measurement data up to the pressure of 7 MPa.
In [1], the TRACE developers reach to the following conclusion regarding the IAFB correlation:

"It also does a reasonable job of correlating the pressure effect observed in the Winfrith data as 
shown in Figure 6-30 though some under-prediction of the high-pressure data for thick vapor films 
is evident."

In [2] the authors proposed a new model, in which they use measurement data up to 9 MPa. In addition 
to that, they found that between 2 MPa and 9 MPa, the effect of the system pressure on the wall HTC 
seems negligible. The authors also suggest that the model of TRACE should be used for low flow rate 
and low pressure cases. 


